WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.460
So what we have is we have a triangle

00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:07.799
The only requirement is that the base angles here are acute.

00:00:07.799 --> 00:00:12.689
And what we want to do is we want to somehow either draw a square,

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:17.879
so I'm just going to roughly sketch it out. In this case

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:22.949
we can see that, you know, we have a rectangle that is slightly wider than it is high

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:23.580
so

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:29.500
How can we get a square into this triangle, using just a compass and straightedge?

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:33.860
that lies on this base and then touches the two sides.

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:40.140
As mathematicians, before we start on actually constructing something, a good question to ask is:

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:41.680
Does this exist?

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:46.379
You know, how do we know we can get exactly a square, instead of just a rectangle?

00:00:46.379 --> 00:00:52.379
So what we're going to do, is actually use this idea of rectangles. So, as mathematicians,

00:00:53.289 --> 00:00:57.149
sometimes we are looking for squares, but we can't find a square,

00:00:57.430 --> 00:01:03.959
so we relax the restriction and we start looking for rectangles and then we hope we can find a rectangle

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:08.489
where the height is equal to the base length, and  then we know we have a square.

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:11.610
So, how can we get rectangles?

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:17.669
Well from the height of this triangle we can just draw any

00:01:19.479 --> 00:01:21.000
parallel line to the base

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:24.570
and then drop the perpendiculars

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:31.470
and we now get a rectangle. We see that this blue rectangle is now a lot higher than it is long.

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:34.349
The green rectangle was a lot longer than it is high

00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:38.500
so we hope that there's a square that lies somewhere in between this range.

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:40.900
(Brady: As you sort slide between the two?)
- Right.

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:47.100
So let's actually draw it out. You know what that looks like, so I'm going to draw a graph.

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:54.479
Actually I'm using a ruler to measure stuff. So this isn't yet a purely straightedge and compass construction

00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:58.559
So we're going to plot the height of the rectangles as they get lower and lower.

00:01:58.930 --> 00:02:02.369
So for now I'm going to use my ruler to draw it out.

00:02:03.009 --> 00:02:05.009
So it is off about

00:02:05.439 --> 00:02:07.439
4 units

00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:10.739
so over here is going to be about four units and

00:02:12.849 --> 00:02:16.469
at the height of zero the height of the rectangle is zero,

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:21.510
so what we have is a straight line here

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:24.720
representing the height of the rectangle so

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:29.910
Now that we have the height of the rectangle, we're going to draw the width of the rectangle.

00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:34.739
When the height is at the full height the width of the rectangle is - zero

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:41.609
So ever point down here and when the height is at zero, we have the full length of the base.

00:02:41.709 --> 00:02:44.939
Which means we're going to have a straight line here.

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:47.730
And.. what happens at this point of intersection?

00:02:48.549 --> 00:02:54.419
Well at this point of intersection, we know that the height of the rectangle is equal to the width of the rectangle

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:58.100
and so that gives us a square.
(Brady: So we can do a square?)

00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:00.100
- So, we can do a square and

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:07.650
when we had the use of ruler to transfer heights and lengths around we could find this is the height at which

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:10.120
the square will occur.

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:16.079
So if we have a proper construction, the hope is that we can draw a square here.

00:03:16.079 --> 00:03:17.100
(Brady: Oh, yeah)

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:24.029
- So we'll see if we actually can match them. Another trick that mathematicians use is that if they can't find a square

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:26.340
they actually force out a square

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:32.880
So, how do I force out a square here, is that it's hard to force a square in there

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:35.729
But I can draw a square down below.

00:03:36.489 --> 00:03:39.719
So using my compass and straightedge I can actually

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:43.079
draw a square and we're going to use this

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:45.519
to

00:03:45.519 --> 00:03:49.679
Approximate a compass. So over here, we want to draw a square

00:03:50.799 --> 00:03:52.799
It's going to come down to here

00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:55.650
over here

00:03:55.930 --> 00:03:57.930
We want to draw a square,

00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:01.300
it's going to come down to - about here.

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:05.440
And so this gives us an approximate square.

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:11.060
And actually I do not need this ruler anymore, so I'm going to put it away and just use a straight edge.

00:04:11.069 --> 00:04:13.069
So we're going to

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:16.410
continue this out

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:19.530
and

00:04:19.530 --> 00:04:25.540
continue this out. And now what we have here, is we have a square, in this large triangle.

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:33.220
So it's not quite the square that we want in this small triangle up here, but at least it's a square in a large triangle.

00:04:33.980 --> 00:04:38.040
What can we say about this small triangle and this large triangle?

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:43.920
Because this edge moves down parallel, these two triangles are actually similar

00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:48.080
So there's a mapping that brings us from this small triangle,

00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:53.280
to this large triangle. And we can ask: "What does the mapping do to this square?"

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:54.440
(Brady: Because you've got the square now?)

00:04:54.440 --> 00:04:59.580
Yes! So think about what happens to the base of this square,

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.760
as we expand the small triangle to the big triangle, and as we contract it back.

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:09.220
This base is actually just going to be a straight line,

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:12.860
through to the vertex of the original triangle.

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:14.500
Likewise over here,

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:20.660
if we connect the vertex to this point, is how this point will move as we contract the triangle

00:05:20.660 --> 00:05:22.900
So now the base of the large square

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:27.420
goes over to these two points. And what that means,

00:05:27.810 --> 00:05:34.190
is that we now have the base of a smaller square in this triangle up here.

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:36.220
We just need to construct the

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:37.920
perpendiculars and

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:45.499
this will give us our square. And as I mentioned earlier we can compare it to this diagram on the right

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:47.690
And we can see that we are

00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:51.230
reasonably close to what the calculator answer is.

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:57.049
Method one, where we constructed the two lines that intersect each other to find the height of the

00:05:57.450 --> 00:06:03.379
square. And method two, where we forced a square to get into this triangle.

00:06:03.570 --> 00:06:06.049
So now we're going to look at a third way -

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:09.440
How else can we relax the restriction of the square?

00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:16.760
You know, instead of forcing all the vertices to line up perfectly on the edge of the triangle,

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:19.220
maybe we could say hey,

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:21.440
let's just have

00:06:25.730 --> 00:06:28.939
the base of the square touching

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:36.360
one side of the triangle. Okay, we can also continue drawing more squares,

00:06:37.460 --> 00:06:39.460
and see what they look like.

00:06:41.850 --> 00:06:45.980
Okay, and so this figure of squares that are touching

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:48.000
the side of this triangle,

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:54.880
we hope we eventually will get to a square that does indeed touch this side of a triangle.

00:06:55.340 --> 00:07:03.140
If we can find such a point we are done. And what we have here, is that we again have our similar triangles.

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:07.020
So we started out with this triangle,

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:12.020
and we moved the base over we got to this triangle.

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:16.520
So what then does that tell us about this point,

00:07:19.100 --> 00:07:19.600
and

00:07:19.820 --> 00:07:26.920
the vertex of this triangle, is that these three points will form a straight line.

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:29.960
So, we could

00:07:29.970 --> 00:07:35.780
connect up these three points, and see where it intersects the other side of the triangle

00:07:35.780 --> 00:07:43.280
and then from here, we know if we draw the perpendicular and we drop the parallel line that will give us a square.

00:07:44.180 --> 00:07:46.900
(Brady: Huh. That's even.. that's almost simpler than the other one.)

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:48.900
That is simpler than the other one

00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:53.020
And you know, both of this use the same idea of similarity.

00:07:53.020 --> 00:07:54.660
[paper crunching noises]

00:07:54.660 --> 00:08:01.760
Alright, so we are back on the first diagram and remember what we wanted to do is start off by constructing a square.

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:04.400
So,

00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:08.320
I'm going to just quickly measure off

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:24.240
And we say, that,

00:08:24.490 --> 00:08:28.349
the vertex of the triangle, and this corner of

00:08:29.140 --> 00:08:32.220
our now relaxed square, we want it to

00:08:33.070 --> 00:08:37.439
we want to extend this line and we can see that - hey, it does! -

00:08:39.160 --> 00:08:44.339
go through this other point on a triangle. That's pretty amazing :D

00:08:44.440 --> 00:08:45.840
(Brady: Look how happy you look. You're very happy with yourself.)
[laughs]

00:08:48.040 --> 00:08:54.680
So we have three different methods here and they all agree on this one point to give us the square,

00:08:54.880 --> 00:09:00.450
we drop the perpendicular, we drop the parallel line, and we form our square in this triangle

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:02.600
[Preview of next video] Get wild

00:09:02.680 --> 00:09:03.180
woohoo

00:09:03.190 --> 00:09:09.660
So it looks like, for any triangle we will have those three segments intersecting in a point

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:16.499
Well that point turns out to be the center of mass, or our point, which also is known the centroid.

